00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:07.470
Hillel, thank you for sitting with me for
this interview.

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:09.000
Pleasure to be here.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:10.909
How can I help you?

00:01:10.909 --> 00:01:15.420
For anyone not familiar with TLA+ can you
give us kind of a summary?

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:16.420
What is TLA+?

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:19.540
So let's actually start with a bit of the
bigger picture first.

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:20.540
Okay.

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:21.540
Software correctness.

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:23.900
We need to be able to write software that
we wanna be able to write it quickly.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:26.950
We want to be able to write cheaply, want
to be able to write it that it's correct,

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:27.950
right?

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:30.850
Generally, there are multiple different kinds
of bugs.

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:34.491
The most common kinds of bugs are what we
might call implementation details, where you

00:01:34.491 --> 00:01:37.170
are essentially writing something and you
mess something up.

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:41.570
You put something in the wrong place, you're
coding something, and you make a small mistake.

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:44.600
Those are the things that we try to catch
with our type systems, our code review, our

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:45.800
testing, et cetera.

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:47.870
I'm sure you're familiar with all of that.

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:51.950
Now there's another class of bugs broadly
that we can think of as design bugs, where

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:56.270
you are implementing something correctly,
but the thing you're implementing, doesn't

00:01:56.270 --> 00:01:58.170
do what you want it to do.

00:01:58.170 --> 00:02:03.091
Maybe it fundamentally has a race condition
just built into the core essence there, or

00:02:03.091 --> 00:02:08.039
maybe it doesn't actually satisfy your requirements
in all cases or maybe there is one execution

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:10.360
which causes to shut down all servers.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:14.040
And you can't really say that that's a bug
because it's actually part of the implementation.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:16.030
It's part of the features.

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:18.980
So these are the bugs that are a lot rarer,
but they're also the ones that are much more

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:19.980
dangerous.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:21.750
They tend to be harder to attack.

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:25.590
They tend to blow up much more nastily in
production and fixing them tends to take a

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:29.350
lot more time because you have to fundamentally
redesign your system.

00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:32.450
The problem with trying to solve these things
with like tests and types and checking and

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:34.950
such as that, those all work on the code level.

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:38.810
They work on an existing implementation and
these are issues you want to find before you

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:43.170
have the implementation so you don't spend
weeks and weeks implementing the wrong thing.

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:47.060
This leads to a class of tools we call formal
specification languages, which are languages

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:50.840
you can use to express abstract models of
a system and catch these design bugs before

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:52.459
you have to start coding them.

00:02:52.459 --> 00:02:57.069
TLA+ is one of the tools in this, and right
now rapidly becoming the most popular one

00:02:57.069 --> 00:03:00.290
due to accessibility and its power.

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:04.430
It was sort of catapulted to the main stage
by Amazon Web Services when they published

00:03:04.430 --> 00:03:08.930
a paper about how they found critical bugs
in S3 and Dynamo DB using this, bugs that

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:14.530
passed all of their current error corrections
systems and only took them like a few weeks.

00:03:14.530 --> 00:03:18.739
A weekend in one case of practice to find
that sort of got a lot more people into it,

00:03:18.739 --> 00:03:23.349
including me, I used it at a small education
startup where it probably saved us months

00:03:23.349 --> 00:03:26.260
and tens or hundreds of thousands of dollars.

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:29.420
And that got me excited to start talking about
it more publicly.

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:37.900
The approach that TLA+ takes for doing, I
guess, specification testing is through expressing

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:41.319
specifications in, the language of TLA+?

00:03:41.319 --> 00:03:42.319
Yes.

00:03:42.319 --> 00:03:45.060
one way to sort of think about that is that
imagine you're trying to implement, I don't

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:47.849
know, like querying a database, right?

00:03:47.849 --> 00:03:52.810
At the really, really high level it's just
data's here, it is now in this value.

00:03:52.810 --> 00:03:55.960
At the actual mutation level, you have all
of your like classes, you have your structures,

00:03:55.960 --> 00:03:59.400
you have this entire control flow, you have
audits, you have error checking, you have

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:00.800
probably a logging in there somewhere.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:04.440
You probably have some kind of dependency
injection, so you can swap in and out different

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:05.440
kinds of database.

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:11.170
And so what could be hundreds or thousands
of lines of implementation code can at the

00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:15.340
really high abstract level be one or two lines
of modeling code.

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:22.220
That actually sounds really remarkable and
I guess I would ask how is it that things

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:28.460
that are, I guess, components of distributed
systems can be represented with such little

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:31.350
amount of modeling code?

00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:34.430
Because fundamentally the idea here is that
we're looking at the design, we're looking

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:39.090
at sort of trying to find these, these less
common, but much more critical issues that

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:42.330
exist at a much higher, more fundamental level
of the system.

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:46.270
So we can essentially abstract away all of
these implementation details, that stuff that's

00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:51.889
necessary to make the system work on computers,
but isn't really part of our abstract machine,

00:04:51.889 --> 00:04:55.040
our essential machine, as you might think
of it,

00:04:55.040 --> 00:05:00.790
Are there any kind of formal specification
languages that TLA+ either builds on or relies

00:05:00.790 --> 00:05:03.560
on or changes or improves upon?

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:04.630
Yes.

00:05:04.630 --> 00:05:07.270
A little bit about the history of like this
entire space.

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:11.360
There was a few different branches that mostly
started, Vienna Development Method of IBM

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:12.850
in I believe the late 1960s.

00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:18.060
And then there was some sort of, some offshoots
of that, primarily Zed, there was a separate

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:23.580
branch called linear temporal logic, which
by which was basically designed to model,

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:26.979
essentially create a mathematical representation
of how things can change.

00:05:26.979 --> 00:05:31.950
And then essentially TLA+ is a synthesis of
one branch of temporal logic called temporal

00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:39.150
logic of actions with ideas from Zed and a
few other places I believe.

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:45.490
So time is a component of the specification,
I guess the focus of the modeling that you

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:47.190
can do with the specification language.

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:52.710
Yeah, the way we sort of think about systems
is that we have all the things the system

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:53.730
can do.

00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:57.550
It could say, send a message, or it could
drop a message or could the queue could be

00:05:57.550 --> 00:06:01.130
reordering the messages or something could
receive a message.

00:06:01.130 --> 00:06:06.180
And then every possible execution of those
actions, every possible like sequence of states

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:09.040
that the system could go through is called
the behavior.

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:13.850
Then there is exists, the set of all possible
behaviors this system can do and what we want

00:06:13.850 --> 00:06:18.780
to guarantee using these languages, by inspecting
the properties, is that every single possible

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:22.130
behavior satisfies our high level requirements.

00:06:22.130 --> 00:06:23.170
Yeah.

00:06:23.170 --> 00:06:25.830
There are other different, there are other
different like models of like how we specify

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:28.060
and like how we represent models of systems.

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:32.130
This is the TLA+ way, which tends to be very
good with, you have to deal with any kind

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:34.229
of concurrency distributed systems.

00:06:34.229 --> 00:06:35.229
It's less good for other things.

00:06:35.229 --> 00:06:38.060
And it's really a matter of choosing what
your problem domain is.

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:44.210
Can you kind of give a, an example of the
kind of flaw that's I guess maybe behavior

00:06:44.210 --> 00:06:49.880
oriented that TLA+ would be able to catch
in a distributed system.

00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:53.580
So in the, in the &amp;quot;Tackling Concurrency
Bugs With TLA+&amp;quot; talk I gave at Strange

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:57.650
Loop, I gave some examples of how we used
it at my old workplace.

00:06:57.650 --> 00:07:01.319
And one of the examples was what's called
the batch installer, where we essentially

00:07:01.319 --> 00:07:07.350
had to be moving a lot of resources across
a lot of different iPads, essentially like

00:07:07.350 --> 00:07:10.310
app resources that we had a finite number
of.

00:07:10.310 --> 00:07:16.720
But we were working through a system where
we moved them through by large batch APIs,

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:22.000
but this third-party system we had to work
through was both eventually consistent and

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:23.000
non idempotent.

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:26.550
So if we essentially sent a message saying,
do this, it would be a while before the physical

00:07:26.550 --> 00:07:30.240
world reflected that reality and a while before
their system reflected that reality.

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:35.030
And also by non-idempotent, it means that
if like we were in that space where we said,

00:07:35.030 --> 00:07:36.030
Hey, do this.

00:07:36.030 --> 00:07:37.400
And we said, Hey, do this again.

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:38.860
It would trigger a memory leak.

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:42.520
If we were lucky would trigger a memory leak,
it could do other much worse things.

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:45.730
So we had to build a system that guaranteed
all resources were eventually in the right

00:07:45.730 --> 00:07:51.920
places while also guaranteeing that we never
sent an, a request that would trigger a memory

00:07:51.920 --> 00:08:00.000
leak by being non-idempotent, given that there
was a delay between when the real world reflected

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:02.300
what their system was and what their system
was telling us.

00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:03.300
I see.

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:04.300
Yeah.

00:08:04.300 --> 00:08:05.530
So a lot of places this can go wrong.

00:08:05.530 --> 00:08:09.620
And, a couple of the sprints before we started
using formal modeling did have major issues

00:08:09.620 --> 00:08:14.139
where in specific circumstances, it can either
break idempotency or break the requirement

00:08:14.139 --> 00:08:17.360
and a resource would just be left on the table.

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:21.360
But we were able to sort of use TLA+ to essentially
create a model of a system that the first

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:24.860
model ended up having like a 19 step error
trace where like these 19 things would go

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:25.860
wrong.

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:29.090
We would like enter this loop where something
would just not get moved around and that we

00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:30.120
can make a fix on that.

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:31.690
And then thefix would turn out to like have
a different issue.

00:08:31.690 --> 00:08:35.099
So we'd rethink like the design and eventually
we were able to find a design after like a

00:08:35.099 --> 00:08:38.639
couple of days of modeling that guaranteed
all the properties we needed.

00:08:38.639 --> 00:08:41.601
It also gave us enough insight into what you
need to do that actually building the system

00:08:41.601 --> 00:08:46.129
ended up taking like a couple of weeks faster
than we expected.

00:08:46.129 --> 00:08:47.129
Hmm.

00:08:47.129 --> 00:08:51.819
So, considering what you were just describing,
can you, I guess, say a little bit about where

00:08:51.819 --> 00:08:55.230
TLA+ fits in a development cycle?

00:08:55.230 --> 00:08:56.230
Yeah.

00:08:56.230 --> 00:08:57.379
So there's a few different ways people use
it.

00:08:57.379 --> 00:09:01.720
This is actually more interesting question
because given that this kind of formal specification

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:05.820
tool sort of doesn't really have any analogs
in mainstream development.

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:07.130
It's a, it's like a very large field.

00:09:07.130 --> 00:09:11.139
There's like a lot of like, isn't it, but
they're not like very common tools.

00:09:11.139 --> 00:09:14.639
And because they solve an issue that really
isn't solved by anything else when people

00:09:14.639 --> 00:09:18.470
sort of do start using these tools, they end
up using them in a lot of different ways that

00:09:18.470 --> 00:09:21.670
they might not necessarily be the best use
of that may not be the best tools to do that,

00:09:21.670 --> 00:09:25.070
but because there's nothing else out there
they're best creatively needs to use them

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:26.350
if that makes sense.

00:09:26.350 --> 00:09:30.830
Fundamentally, there's a couple of places
that are considered the &amp;quot;proper&amp;quot;

00:09:30.830 --> 00:09:31.830
place to use them.

00:09:31.830 --> 00:09:35.779
So for example, before you start like writing
a large amount of code, you model out the

00:09:35.779 --> 00:09:37.360
systems we're going to build.

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:40.889
It's also often used when you're trying to
refactor existing code or like finding a bug

00:09:40.889 --> 00:09:44.500
where you create a model of the system to
sort of figure out better where the bug is

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:47.579
and then make the changes you expect to make
sure that doesn't reintroduce a different

00:09:47.579 --> 00:09:51.980
bug That said, because these tools kind of
do something that nobody really otherwise

00:09:51.980 --> 00:09:55.100
has access to people end up using them in
a lot of different ways, sort of pushing the

00:09:55.100 --> 00:09:56.589
limits of how they can use them.

00:09:56.589 --> 00:10:01.100
For example, one of the earliest like creative
misuses I found was on documenting things

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:05.129
where you'd essentially take a system and
write an executable documentation of the entire

00:10:05.129 --> 00:10:08.720
system and show how making changes would cause
different impacts.

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:12.339
I've also seen it used for people doing optimization,
where they prove an optimization would not

00:10:12.339 --> 00:10:16.139
like introduce new bugs to have identical
behavior before actually like implementing

00:10:16.139 --> 00:10:17.139
the optimization.

00:10:17.139 --> 00:10:22.239
I have seen people use it as a constraint
solver or as like a model space generator

00:10:22.239 --> 00:10:25.299
where they essentially use it to just build
out a state space and sort of see what the

00:10:25.299 --> 00:10:26.829
state space looks like.

00:10:26.829 --> 00:10:28.470
So there's a lot different ways you can use
it.

00:10:28.470 --> 00:10:32.730
But the ways that the majority people use
it are either are basically before you start

00:10:32.730 --> 00:10:33.730
implementation.

00:10:33.730 --> 00:10:40.649
You did though describe the use of TLA+ in,
I guess, a setting once you, either, once

00:10:40.649 --> 00:10:49.449
you have a, a working system or, maybe some
way into the development cycle for a system.

00:10:49.449 --> 00:10:57.389
I remember either from a talk that you've
given or maybe from your book, how specifications

00:10:57.389 --> 00:11:04.439
that are expressed with TLA+ are completely
separate from, the application code itself,

00:11:04.439 --> 00:11:06.389
it's just a completely different thing.

00:11:06.389 --> 00:11:10.120
And it doesn't, it's not meant to drive the
application.

00:11:10.120 --> 00:11:11.120
Right.

00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:16.559
It's just meant to represent states that a
system can be in given a model that describes

00:11:16.559 --> 00:11:18.920
how the systems behaviors work.

00:11:18.920 --> 00:11:19.920
Right.

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:26.749
So I, I guess one question I have is, how
is it that teams that use TLA+ can try to

00:11:26.749 --> 00:11:32.750
keep the models that they use to specify a
system and the system itself.

00:11:32.750 --> 00:11:39.069
How do those things remain in sync when you
have a working system, either when you have

00:11:39.069 --> 00:11:45.060
a working system that TLA+ was used to sort
of design at the behaviors of, or, when you

00:11:45.060 --> 00:11:50.580
have a working system and you're sort of starting
from scratch, but you get the model of specification

00:11:50.580 --> 00:11:56.179
and the application itself sort of in sync,
but over time, the system changes and grows.

00:11:56.179 --> 00:12:00.569
How do those things, like what are the ways
that people try to keep those things in sync?

00:12:00.569 --> 00:12:03.160
Code review, lots and lots of code review.

00:12:03.160 --> 00:12:07.579
Okay, more or less facetious then that, that
is one of the core limitations of these kinds

00:12:07.579 --> 00:12:10.880
of tools is that there's no easy way to connect
them to the code.

00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:11.880
This is often intentional.

00:12:11.880 --> 00:12:15.519
It gives you more flexibility and makes these
tools much easier to use.

00:12:15.519 --> 00:12:20.529
And it's generally better to get people using
a tool that is say, 10% of the effort, but

00:12:20.529 --> 00:12:24.089
only 50% of the effectiveness versus somethingi
that's like a hundred times the effort and

00:12:24.089 --> 00:12:29.119
twice the effectiveness kind of thing that
said there are techniques that people do use.

00:12:29.119 --> 00:12:32.620
One of the common ones that I've seen and
I've done myself is what's called I'm not

00:12:32.620 --> 00:12:36.699
sure there's actually a proper term for this,
but I sort of think of it as trace testing.

00:12:36.699 --> 00:12:39.149
So what you can do is create your model, right?

00:12:39.149 --> 00:12:41.730
And the model is set, essentially creates
a state space, right?

00:12:41.730 --> 00:12:44.160
Of like everything that can happen in the
system.

00:12:44.160 --> 00:12:49.549
And then what you can do is you can turn that
into say a JSON stream of actions and create

00:12:49.549 --> 00:12:55.269
what's called an orchestrator on your code
that reads in this JSON and makes the corresponding

00:12:55.269 --> 00:12:59.790
moves in your system, say it sees, okay, so
this writer will do this thing.

00:12:59.790 --> 00:13:03.870
So we all send a message to this writer and
have it make this like operation.

00:13:03.870 --> 00:13:08.050
Then essentially what happens is you then
check that what the system does it, what?

00:13:08.050 --> 00:13:13.970
Sorry, what your code does matches what the
abstract TLA+ model does modulo like translation

00:13:13.970 --> 00:13:15.791
between the data layers.

00:13:15.791 --> 00:13:19.670
I've seen this actually used really, really
effectively at some places, the main downside

00:13:19.670 --> 00:13:23.750
is that you kind of have to handroll for the
particular system you're working on so we

00:13:23.750 --> 00:13:27.879
can't make any general third-party libraries
or at least nobody has.

00:13:27.879 --> 00:13:28.879
Yeah.

00:13:28.879 --> 00:13:36.579
I guess figuring out a way for, the specification
to either drive system level tests, or to

00:13:36.579 --> 00:13:41.499
use the specification to confirm that the
system behaves as the model expects it to,

00:13:41.499 --> 00:13:47.359
I feel like those things would be like having
some, some approach for, tackling those problems

00:13:47.359 --> 00:13:53.919
would help teams use the tool in a way so
that there's not a lot of divergence between

00:13:53.919 --> 00:13:54.919
the two, right?.

00:13:54.919 --> 00:13:58.939
I should, emphasize though that while divergence
can happen, it's also, it can be solved at

00:13:58.939 --> 00:14:04.120
the human level with like process better processes
and more fundamentally, I think it is important

00:14:04.120 --> 00:14:08.009
to note that while this isn't like, well,
this would take like what we can do with this

00:14:08.009 --> 00:14:11.839
stuff to the next level, what we can do currently,
even with like code and spec diverging is

00:14:11.839 --> 00:14:17.279
still much, much, much more valuable than
we can do without it.

00:14:17.279 --> 00:14:18.990
It would make it, it would make a good thing
great.

00:14:18.990 --> 00:14:22.709
It wouldn't make an unacceptable thing acceptable,
if that makes sense.

00:14:22.709 --> 00:14:23.720
Mm.

00:14:23.720 --> 00:14:24.730
Yeah.

00:14:24.730 --> 00:14:30.629
So, as I've been thinking about TLA+, like
one of the things that occurred to me was,

00:14:30.629 --> 00:14:37.189
whether TLA+ is either like a proofer or a
compiler for, state machines that represent

00:14:37.189 --> 00:14:38.189
distributed systems.

00:14:38.189 --> 00:14:40.079
Would you say that that that's a fair analogy?

00:14:40.079 --> 00:14:41.980
Um, I would not.

00:14:41.980 --> 00:14:45.220
So the first thing I wanna mention here is
that there is actually a language that sort

00:14:45.220 --> 00:14:49.709
of you write specifications and it compiles
them to actual state machine code.

00:14:49.709 --> 00:14:55.329
The most famous in this category is actually
P as in P, as in Papa, by Microsoft research,

00:14:55.329 --> 00:15:00.199
which essentially creates C-sharp code based
on abstract set state machine specifications,

00:15:00.199 --> 00:15:04.350
TLA+ itself is just essentially a notation
for describing systems.

00:15:04.350 --> 00:15:10.079
Then we have model checkers or proof systems
that then take those models of the system

00:15:10.079 --> 00:15:12.589
and verify that they have properties.

00:15:12.589 --> 00:15:17.579
So I guess the, the main, the main like thing
I actually want to relate it to is Z3, but

00:15:17.579 --> 00:15:21.110
that's probably not very useful either because
that's also like a tool in this space.

00:15:21.110 --> 00:15:26.029
So I guess maybe the best way to describe
it would essentially be like a configuration

00:15:26.029 --> 00:15:30.259
language that generates a test suite.

00:15:30.259 --> 00:15:34.149
But that's like, honestly, I it's honestly
hard to come up with good analogies here because

00:15:34.149 --> 00:15:37.269
this is like a tool that like does something
we don't really do otherwise.

00:15:37.269 --> 00:15:40.949
So like all the analogies in some way end
up breaking down a bit,

00:15:40.949 --> 00:15:45.160
Yeah, that's, that's not uncommon, right,
where you've got analogies that are leaky

00:15:45.160 --> 00:15:50.929
in one way or another to try to just communicate
an idea, especially one that's sort of novel

00:15:50.929 --> 00:15:53.549
and unlike other things, too.

00:15:53.549 --> 00:15:57.269
Where is it that folks can learn more about
TLA+?

00:15:57.269 --> 00:16:02.999
So, first of all, if you are coming to Strange
Loop, we still have like space open for, the

00:16:02.999 --> 00:16:04.959
TLA+ conference, which happens the day before,

00:16:04.959 --> 00:16:07.079
So there are essentially three resources.

00:16:07.079 --> 00:16:10.470
Actually before I did, um, the Strange Loop
talk, I was so excited about this that I wrote

00:16:10.470 --> 00:16:15.870
an online documentation, learntla.com. that
one I was updating from about like 2017 to

00:16:15.870 --> 00:16:16.870
2018.

00:16:16.870 --> 00:16:20.119
I'm currently a little bit unsatisfied with
it and both, but I haven't had to update it

00:16:20.119 --> 00:16:25.279
because afterwards I got a book deal to write
Practical TLA+, and I think this is currently

00:16:25.279 --> 00:16:28.690
the best introduction to TLA+, a lot of pedagogy
went into it.

00:16:28.690 --> 00:16:35.100
It is full of practical examples of how to
write, basically how to write specs, how to

00:16:35.100 --> 00:16:38.310
actually apply it to business problems, how
to use it for like modeling requirements or

00:16:38.310 --> 00:16:41.749
distributed systems or data structures, et
cetera.

00:16:41.749 --> 00:16:43.329
I currently think that's the strongest one.

00:16:43.329 --> 00:16:46.089
I don't think it'll be the strongest as of
2022, because I'm going to take the stuff

00:16:46.089 --> 00:16:49.800
I learned in the past four years to like,
completely revamp learntla.com and I'm scheduled

00:16:49.800 --> 00:16:52.749
to start doing that in my planner, starting
2022.

00:16:52.749 --> 00:16:56.779
Um, and then finally, if you want to sort
of go to the source and like learn the canonical

00:16:56.779 --> 00:17:00.720
text to like learn fundamentally what's going
on versus something more focused on getting

00:17:00.720 --> 00:17:04.850
you applying it, there are &amp;quot;Specifying
Systems&amp;quot; by Leslie Lamport.

00:17:04.850 --> 00:17:09.339
This is actually free too, if you download
the TLA+ Toolbox, it is included as a free

00:17:09.339 --> 00:17:13.720
PDF and it's a little bit more advanced and
I think it requires a bit more like mathematical,

00:17:13.720 --> 00:17:17.949
like comfort with mathematical ability, and
approaches it sort of in a bit of a different

00:17:17.949 --> 00:17:22.899
way, but it also like is the reference text
that people use to like, understand how the

00:17:22.899 --> 00:17:27.480
system works and like how to resolve certain
questions.

00:17:27.480 --> 00:17:31.080
So Hillel, I want to thank you for sitting
with me for this interview.

00:17:31.080 --> 00:17:36.700
It's been really enjoyable talking to you,
for anyone watching this video as I mentioned

00:17:36.700 --> 00:17:40.549
at the beginning, if you're interested in
Strange Loop conference, tickets are available

00:17:40.549 --> 00:17:43.480
for both in-person and virtual attendance.

00:17:43.480 --> 00:17:45.980
workshop day for the conference is going to
be Thursday, September 30th.

00:17:45.980 --> 00:17:48.820
That's the same day as the TLA+ conference.

00:17:48.820 --> 00:17:51.799
And I would like to point, and I would like
to point out that if you're not going to the

00:17:51.799 --> 00:17:56.540
TLA+ conference, Saul Pwanson is going to
